{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>From good to evil.</b> And as for what is in between, that means things that can be understood in two ways. Accordingly, He forbade him three kinds of speech. First, He forbade him to speak with him 'from good' — good things and consolations, for the goodness of the wicked is evil to the righteous, because in any case the heart of the wicked intends harm to the righteous, and when he speaks kindly with him, surely all his words are in the manner of hypocrisy, so that the righteous man will not know to beware and to guard himself from him. And this matter is self-evident — that you shall not speak with him, for through this one may come to harm. But even middle speech that is susceptible to two interpretations — you might have thought that it is permitted for you to speak with him, and he should have set it upon his heart and at least been cautious — it teaches us through the word 'עַד,' to tell you that it is forbidden for you to mislead him with words. And not only these two kinds of speech are forbidden to you, because through them the righteous man might come to stumbling — but even outright evil: you might have thought that it is permitted for you to exaggerate to him with evil words so that he will be on guard and protect himself from him, and if he is not on guard, I have saved my own soul — it teaches us 'רָע,' to tell you: you shall not exaggerate to him, nor do him any evil." "And with this one resolves what is said nearby: ״וְאַתָּה לֹא תַעֲבֹר אֶת הַגַּל הַזֶּה אֵלַי לְרָעָה״ (Genesis 31:52) — this implies: but for good you may cross. And conversely, Laban said: ״אִם אָנִי לֹא אֶעֱבֹר אֵלֶיךָ אֶת הַגַּל״ (Genesis 31:52) — this implies: in no case shall I cross, even for good. And this is because my goodness is worthless to you, for last night Elohim already said to me: ״הִשָּׁמֶר לְךָ מִדַּבֵּר עִם יַעֲקֹב מִטּוֹב״ (Genesis 31:29) — hear from this that he does not desire my goodness, for I am suspect in his eyes, that all my kindness toward you is for evil. And he said: ״אִם אָנִי לֹא אֶעֱבֹר אֵלֶיךָ אֶת הַגַּל הַזֶּה״ — even if I do not cross also the pillar, but ״אַתָּה לֹא תַעֲבֹר אֵלַי הַגַּל וְהַמַּצֵּבָה לְרָעָה״ (Genesis 31:52) — this implies: but the mound alone, without crossing the pillar, is permitted even for evil, because in between, surely Jacob would be appeased regarding the evil — which is not so with Laban. And this plain sense is correct."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {1 "The verse citation in the second comment appears as Genesis 31:29; in the canonical text, the divine warning to Laban is at Genesis 31:24, while Genesis 31:29 contains Laban's own reference to that warning. The Kli Yakar cites the latter address where Laban quotes it; retained as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:31:26.897303618Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}